        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Microorganisms are ubiquitous in spacecraft environments, as they are on Earth. Microbes pose several risks to humans in space including: infectious diseases, allergies, degradation of air quality (e.g., release of volatiles), release of toxins, degradation of critical materials, and systems failure (e.g., water reclamation system). Maintaining the health and performance of humans in closed microgravity environments while reusing reclaimed air and water is a daunting task. Determining medical and technological risks that are dependent on microbiological factors during long-term space flight is a vital problem in theory, as well as practice. In such a closed environment, humans will be a major source of microbes released into the spacecraft. Animals and plants, when present, will also be significant contributors to the overall bioburden. Suitable containment and purification technologies are essential. Studies of microbial ecology in spacecraft have been mostly limited to short (<30 days) duration missions in the US program. Similar studies have been conducted during long-duration missions in the Russian program. The Mir space station provided an opportunity for a relatively comprehensive US-Russian joint study of the crewmembers and the environment of the Mir.</p>
<p>The success of long-duration space missions will depend on our ability to mitigate the adverse effects of microorganisms. This will require a much better understanding of the microbiology of closed environments and the human-microbe-environment interactions than are currently available. This joint US-Russian investigation was an important first step in gaining this understanding.</p>
<p>The investigators hypothesized that during long-term space flight (>30 days) human microbiota and the microbial ecology of the Mir station will undergo quantitative and qualitative changes. The objectives of this study were to 1) characterize microbiota of crewmembers, air, surface, and water before, during, and after a long-duration mission aboard the Mir Space Station, 2) determine the exchange and distribution of microbes among crewmembers, and 3) assess the distribution and dynamics of crew microbiota throughout the Mir Space Station.</p>
<p>APPROACH:<br>
Crew microbiology <br />
Pre and postflight, microbial samples from the throat, nose, ear, hand, scapula, axilla, groin, feces, and urine were collected and examined for bacteria and fungi. Feces were also examined for ova and parasites. In-flight microbial samples from the throat, nose, ear, hand, scapula, axilla, and groin were collected with the Crew Microbiology Kit (CMK). Quantification and isolation of organisms was performed by, preparing serial dilutions of each sample and spread-plating those samples onto selective media. Any aerobic bacteria or fungi isolated were identified using a Vitek Automated Identification System, Biolog Automated Identification System, or classical means. Species that were not identified by these means were sent to a microbial reference laboratory for identification.</p>
<p>Air microbiology <br />
Air samples were collected from the Shuttle middeck and flightdeck, pre, in, and postflight using the Microbial Air Sampler (MAS). In-flight air samples were also collected from and Mir with the MAS. Mir sampling locations included the area of the Commander's seat (Core Module), area of the dining table (Core Module), in the Commander's cabin (Core Module), and in the Kristall Module hatch. Organisms were isolated using selective media, and species were identified as described in the crew microbiology section.</p>
<p>Surface microbiology <br />
Surface samples were collected from the Shuttle pre, in, and post flight. Shuttle sample areas included the half-moon shaped air vent above the Waste Control System in the middeck, the left-most air return in the port side crawl-through, and the half-moon shaped air vent for the Commander's chair in the flight deck. In-flight surface samples were collected from the Shuttle and Mir with the Surface Sampler Kit (SSK). Mir sampling locations included the Commander's seat post #1 (Core Module), the dining table (Core Module), the Commander's cabin wall (Core Module), the right handle of the treadmill (Core Module), and the wall above the hatch in the Kristall Module. Once during the NASA 3 mission, a sample from the galley water dispenser needle was collected. Organisms were isolated using selective media, and species were identified as described in the crew microbiology section.</p>
<p>Water microbiology <br />
Water samples were collected from the Shuttle pre and postflight. Tank A was sampled preflight, and tanks A and B were sampled postflight. In-flight water samples from the Mir were collected and processed with the Water Experiment Kit (WEK). Mir sampling locations included the galley hot and coldwater ports, and the SVO-VZ port. Organisms were isolated using selective media, and species were identified as described in the crew microbiology section.</p>
<p>Food packet microbiology <br />
During the NASA 4 mission, a food packet was contaminated due to a puncture in the packaging material. A sample was obtained and processed similar to the procedure used for the shuttle surface sample.</p>
<p>'Free' surface sample <br />
During NASA 6 and 7, crewmembers came in contact with 'free' surface condensate in various locations during housekeeping procedures. Representative samples were collected for evaluation of any potential risks that the crewmembers might be exposed to. The samples were collected using the Contingency Water Quality Kit (CWQK), this being assembled from hardware components contained within the Water Experiment Kit (WEK). The condensates were aspirated into a 60cc syringe, and then expelled into a 300 ml sample bag. These samples were returned to ground for complete analyses. Organisms were isolated using selective media, and species were identified as described in the crew microbiology section.</p>
<p>RESULTS:<br>
CREW MICROBIOLOGY <br />
NASA 3/Mir 22 <br />
6 subjects were sampled pre and post flight, three of which were also sampled in-flight. No significant changes were apparent in the aerobic bacteria isolated from the pre-, in-, and postflight specimens. Staphylococcus aureus was the bacterial species used to follow microbial dissemination from crewmember to crewmember and throughout the Mir environment. Crewmembers 1 and 2 were the prime crewmembers that were replaced one week before flight; <i>S. aureus</i> was recovered only once from the nose on each of these crewmembers. <i>Candida albicans</i> was also recovered once from the throat of Crewmember 2. S. aureus was recovered from the throat of Crewmember 3 twice preflight and once postflight. This bacterium was also recovered from the nose of this crewmember at all preflight sampling sessions, three of four in-flight sessions, and two of three postflight sessions; the organism was occasionally isolated from this person's skin, urine, and feces samples. <i>Candida albicans</i>, an endogenous yeast, was isolated from the feces of Crewmember 3 once postflight. <i>S. aureus</i> was recovered from the throat, nose, hand, and axilla of Crewmember 4 at L-2 days. <i>Enterobacter cloacae</i>, <i>Aeromonas hydrophila</i>, and <i>Enterobacter hormaechei</i> were isolated from the throat of Crewmember 4 at L-2 days; <i>E. cloacae</i> were also isolated from the nose at this sampling time. Yeast was present in 5 of 9 samples collected from Crewmember 4 at R+0; <i>Serratia liquefaciens</i> was isolated from the hand at this collection period. Specimens from Crewmember 5 frequently had <i>S. aureus</i> isolated; is was present in the nose at 6 of 11 sampling periods. Once during flight, the organism was present in 4 of 7 samples. At R+0, it was recovered from 4 of 9 specimens, from 5 of 9 at R+7, and from 5 of 9 at R+11. At L-59, <i>Klebsiella pneumoniae</i> was recovered from the throat of Crewmember 5; <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> was isolated from the throat at L-2. <i>S. aureus</i> was not recovered from Crewmember 6.</p>
<p>NASA 4 <br />
One crewmember was sampled pre, in, and postflight. Opportunistic gram negative rods were recovered from the throat at several sampling times: <i>Panteola agglomerans</i> on FD19, <i>Pastuerella multicoida</i>, <i>Serratia odifera</i>, <i>Serratia species</i>, <i>Enterobacter gergoviae</i> on FD117, and <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> on landing day. <i>Candida albicans</i>, an endogenous yeast, was isolated from the throat 143 days before flight. <i>Klebsiella pneumoniae</i> was recovered from the nose at landing. Organisms recovered from the hand included <i>Acinetobacter haemolyticus</i> on L-9 and <i>Enterococcus faecalis</i> and <i>Serratia marcescens</i> on FD117. <i>Candida albicans</i> was recovered from the groin on FD117, and was present in the feces before and after flight. <i>Enterococcus faecalis</i> was present in low numbers in the urine on landing day and 5 days thereafter.</p>
<p>NASA 5/Mir 24 <br />
3 crewmembers were scheduled to be sampled pre, in, and postflight. The in-flight monitoring that was to be performed on all crewmembers on FD15, FD55, FD85, FD115, and FD135, was cancelled due to the Spektr accident. The sample collection kits, as well as archived samples, were stowed within the Spektr module at the time of the accident. No further sampling or the return of samples was possible. A number of opportunistic gram negative rods were recovered from 5 of 9 samples collected from Crewmember 1 at the L-10 sampling period. <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i>, a potential pathogen, was recovered from the nose of Crewmember 1 at all postflight sampling periods; this organism was also recovered from the throat at R+0. <br />
Several opportunistic gram negative rods were recovered from 4 of 9 samples collected from Crewmember 2 at the L-11 day sampling period. <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> was recovered from the nose of Crewmember 2 at all sampling periods; this organism was also recovered from the throat and groin at R+0. <i>Candida species</i>, an endogenous yeast, was isolated from the throat at all three postflight sampling periods. <br />
Opportunistic gram negative rods were only occasional recovered from Crewmember 3. <i>Klebsiella pnuemoniae</i> was isolated from the groin at R+0 and from the nose at R+14 days. <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> was recovered from the nose of Crewmember 3 at L-28 days; this organism was recovered from the throat and hand at R+14 days. <i>Candida species</i>, was isolated from the throat and feces during both preflight and postflight sampling periods.</p>
<p>NASA 6/Mir 25 <br />
3 subjects were sampled pre and postflight. The opportunistic bacteria <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> as well as <i>Candida albicans</i> (a yeast) were recovered from the throat of Crewmember 1 during both of the preflight sampling times. <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> was also recovered from the nose of this crewmember at all three sampling times. <i>Enterobacter cloacae</i> were isolated from the throat of Crewmember 1 at L-21; at L-14, <i>Candida albicans</i> was recovered from the feces. <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> was recovered from the throat of Crewmember 2 on landing day and from the nose at the L-9 and R+0 sampling times; <i>Candida albicans</i> was isolated from the feces on L-9 and was too numerous to count in the throat at R+0. On landing day, Crewmember 2 exhibited a colony count of too numerous to count from the urine; the organism recover from this specimen was <i>Staphylococcus species</i> and was probably a contaminant. <i>Enterobacter aerogenes</i> was isolated from the throat and nose of Crewmember 3 on landing day. <i>Candida albicans</i> was recovered from this crewmember's throat at L-9 and R+0 (too numerous to count), and from the urine at R+0. <i>Penicillium species</i> (too numerous to count) and <i>Candida parapsilosis</i> were also present in the nose of Crewmember 3 on landing day. <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> is frequently a resident of the nasal passages; <i>Candida species</i> are ubiquitous yeasts, being found as normal flora of the alimentary tract and mucocutaneous membranes.</p>
<p>NASA 7 <br />
One crewmember was sampled pre and postflight. The opportunistic bacteria <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> was recovered from the nose at all three sampling times. No clinical symptoms were experienced by the crewmember as a result of the presence of this organism. <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> is frequently a resident of the nasal passages.</p>
<p>AIR MICROBIOLOGY <br />
NASA 3 and STS-79 <br />
Fungal levels were often above the Russian standard of 100 CFU/m3. Mir bacterial levels occasionally exceeded the Russian standard of 500 CFU/m3. <i>Enterococcus faecalis</i> was isolated from the area of the dining table and in the Commander's cabin on FD52. On FD80, both bacterial and fungal levels in three of four Mir sampling locations greatly exceeded the US standard of 1000 CFU/m3 proposed for the International Space Station (ISS). <br />
The highest bacterial level observed during any of the Shuttle sampling sessions was 319 CFU/m3, found in the middeck of STS-79 at R+0. The highest fungal level observed on the Shuttle was 550 CFU/m3 in the flight deck of STS-79 at R+0.</p>
<p>NASA 4 and STS-81 <br />
On board the Mir in the area near the commander's chair, the bacteria <i>Aeromonas hydrophila</i> and <i>Comamonas testosteroni</i> were recovered on FD53. Bacteria recovered from the area of the dining table included <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> on FD80 and <i>Pseudomonas putida</i> on FD117; fungi isolated from this same area included <i>Aspergillus niger</i> and two species of the yeast <i>Candida</i>. The bacterium <i>Aeromonas veronii biovar sobria</i> was isolated from the commander's cabin on FD19 and FD80; <i>Sphingobacterium spiritovorum</i> and <i>Chryseobacterium meningosepticum</i> were also recovered on FD80. <i>Candida guilliermondii</i> was recovered in the Kristall Module hatch on FD117; <i>Sphingomonas paucimobilis</i> and <i>Candida fumata</i> were isolated during the Shuttle docked phase of STS-84. Mir bacterial levels occasionally exceeded the Russian standard of 500 CFU/m3. Fungal levels were above the Russian standard of 100 CFU/m3 in all sampling locations at all sampling times. The standard set by the US for the International Space Station (ISS) is a combined bacterial and fungal count of 1000 CFU/ m3 . This amount was only occasionally exceeded, e.g. in the area of the commander's seat on FD80, in the area of the dining table on FD117, and in the Kristall Module hatch on FD80 and FD117. Onboard the Shuttle, the fungal levels increased during the docked phases of STS-81.</p>
<p>NASA 5 and STS-84 <br />
The in-flight monitoring that was to be performed on the Mir on FD55, FD85, FD115, and FD135, was cancelled due to the Spektr accident. The sample collection kits, as well as archived samples, were stowed within the Spektr module at the time of the accident. No further sampling or the return of samples was possible. The in-flight recording of data from Mir samples collected on FD19 was received. <br />
On board the Mir, <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> was recovered from the area of the dining table during the docked phase of STS-84; <i>Sphingomonas paucimobilis</i> and <i>Candida famata</i> were isolated from the area of the Kristall module during this same sampling session. On FD19, Mir bacterial levels slightly exceeded the Russian standard of 500 CFU/m3 in 3 of the four sampling sites. Fungal levels were above the Russian standard of 100 CFU/m3 in all sampling locations at all sampling times. The standard set by the US for the International Space Station (ISS) is a combined bacterial and fungal count of 1000 CFU/ m3 . This amount was only occasionally exceeded, e.g. in the area of the commander's seat and in the Commander's cabin on FD19. Onboard the Shuttle, the fungal levels increased during the docked phase of STS-84. <br />
 </p>
<p>NASA 6 and STS-86 <br />
On board the Mir, in the area of the dining table, the fungi <i>Aspergillus niger</i> was isolated on FD29. <i>Bacillus cereus</i> was recovered from this location of FD 54 and FD100; <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> recovered on FD100. Fungi recovered from the area of the commander's cabin included <i>Aspergillus niger</i> on FD54. This same fungi was also recovered in the Kristall Module hatch on FD54. Mir bacterial levels occasionally exceeded the Russian standard of 500 CFU/m3. Fungal levels were always above the Russian standard of 100 CFU/m3. The standard set by the US for the International Space Station (ISS) is a combined bacterial and fungal count of 1000 CFU/ m3 . This amount was only exceeded once in the area of the Commander's seat on FD54 and once in the area of the dining table on FD29. <br />
Onboard the Shuttle, the bacterial and fungal levels exceeded acceptability limits in the Middeck on FD5 of STS-86 during the docked phase. <i>Aspergillus niger</i> was recovered in low numbers from the Flight Deck during the pre-docked phase of STS-86. <br />
 </p>
<p>NASA 7, STS-89, and STS-91 <br />
On board the Mir in the area near the commander's chair, the fungi <i>Aspergillus niger</i> was isolated; yeast recovered from this location included <i>Rhodotorula rubra</i> on FD43, <i>Candida guilliermondii</i> on FD 119, and an unidentified yeast on FD 135. Bacteria recovered from the area of the dining table included <i>Pseudomonas fluorescens</i> on FD119. Bacteria isolated from the commander's cabin included <i>Serratia marcescens</i> on FD78, <i>Stenotrophomonas maltophilia<i> on FD119, and <i>Pasteurella haemolytica</i> on FD135. <i>Aspergillus niger</i> was recovered in the Kristall Module hatch on FD135; The bacterium <i>Serratia liquefaciens</i> was recovered from all of the locations during multiple sampling periods; these included site 4 on FD 43, sites 1,2, and 4 on FD78, and from all sampling sites on FD 119. Mir bacterial levels occasionally exceeded the Russian standard of 500 CFU/m3. Fungal levels were frequently above the Russian standard of 100 CFU/m3. The standard set by the US for the International Space Station (ISS) is a combined bacterial and fungal count of 1000 CFU/ m3. This amount was only exceeded once in the Kristall Module hatch on FD43. <br />
Onboard the shuttle, the fungus <i>Aspergillus niger</i> was isolated during the pre-docked phase of STS-89 in the Flight Deck. The yeast <i>Candida guilliermondii</i> was isolated during the docked phase of STS-91 in the Middeck; however, the bacterial and fungal levels never exceeded acceptability limits. </i></i></p>
<p>SURFACE MICROBIOLOGY <br />
NASA 3 and STS-79 <br />
Mir bacterial levels occasionally exceeded both US and Russian standards; fungal levels exceeded the standards only once during the mission. S. aureus was isolated from the Commander's seat (post #1) on FD24. Yeasts were isolated from the dining table on FD24, FD52, and FD80 of the NASA 3 mission, as well as during the docked phase of STS-81. During the docked phase with STS-79 and on FD52, yeast was recovered from the wall in the Commander's cabin. In the Kristall Module hatch, yeast was isolated on FD109; <i>Serratia liquefaciens</i> was found during the docked phase of STS-81. <br />
Both bacterial and fungal levels during all of the sampling sessions for STS-79 were within the US limits proposed for ISS. The yeast <i>Cryptococcus albidus</i> was isolated from the half-moon shaped air vent above the Waste Control System in the middeck at both preflight sampling periods of STS-79; an unidentified yeast was isolated from the same location during the docked phase with Mir. No yeast was recovered from any location after landing.</p>
<p>NASA 4 and STS-81 <br />
Mir bacterial levels frequently exceeded the Russian standard of 1000 CFU/100 cm2 as well as the US standard of 4000 CFU/100 cm2 set for ISS. Fungal levels were occasionally above the Russian standard of 100 CFU/100 cm2, but exceeded the US standard of 400 CFU/100 cm2 set for ISS only once. Bacteria recovered from the commander's seat post #1 included <i>Sphingomonas paucimobilis</i> on FD19, <i>Aeromonas veronii biovar sobria</i>, <i>Pseudomonas putida</i>, and an unidentified gram negative rod on FD53, as well as an unidentified gram negative rod on FD80. <i>Aeromonas veronii biovar sobria</i> was isolated at 4 of the 6 sampling periods from the dining table; other microorganisms recovered from this location were <i>Rhodotorula rubra</i> (a yeast) during the Shuttle docked phase of STS-81, <i>Aspergillus niger</i> on FD19, <i>Aeromonas hydrophila</i> on FD53, <i>Candida parapsilosis</i> on FD117, and <i>Sphingomonas paucimobilis</i> during the Shuttle docked phase of STS-84. From the commander's cabin wall, <i>Aeromonas veronii biovar sobria</i> was again isolated at 4 of the 6 sampling periods; other microorganisms recovered from this location were <i>Aeromonas caviae</i>, <i>Sphingomonas paucimobilis</i>, and <i>Vibrio alganolyticus</i> on FD19, <i>Sphingomonas paucimobilis</i> on FD80, <i>Stenotrophomonas maltophilia</i> and an unidentified gram negative rod on FD117, and <i>Aspergillus niger</i> during the Shuttle docked phase of STS-84. Bacteria isolated from the handle of the treadmill included <i>Aeromonas veronii biovar sobria</i> on FD53 and <i>Chryseomonas luteola</i> on FD80. <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> was recovered from the Kristall Module hatch on FD117 and during the Shuttle docked phase of STS-84. Bacterium also recovered from this location were <i>Serratia liquefaciens</i> and <i>Stenotrophomonas maltophilia</i> during the during the Shuttle docked phase of STS-81, <i>Aeromonas veronii biovar sobria</i> on FD19, <i>Aeromonas caviae</i> on FD53, and <i>Sphingomonas paucimobilis</i> during the Shuttle docked phase of STS-84. On STS-81, both bacterial and fungal levels exceeded US limits at R+0 at the same air vent.</p>
<p>NASA 5 and STS-84 <br />
The in-flight monitoring that was to be performed on the Mir on FD55, FD85, FD115, and FD135was cancelled due to the Spektr accident. The sample collection kits, as well as archived samples, were stowed within the Spektr module at the time of the accident. No further sampling or the return of samples was possible. The in-flight recording of data from Mir samples collected on FD19 was received. Mir bacterial levels frequently exceeded the Russian standard of 1000 CFU/100 cm2 as well as the US standard of 4000 CFU/100 cm2 set for ISS. Fungal levels were occasionally above the Russian standard of 100 CFU/100 cm2, but never exceeded the US standard of 400 CFU/100 cm2 set for ISS. On FD5, <i>Sphingomonas paucimobilis</i> was recovered from the dining table and Kristall module hatch; <i>Aeromonas veronii biovar sobria</i> was recovered from the dining table and the commander's cabin wall. <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> was also isolated on FD5 from the dining table and Kristall module hatch.</p>
<p>NASA 6 and STS-86 <br />
Mir bacterial levels occasionally exceeded the Russian standard of 1000 CFU/100 cm2 as well as the US standard of 4000 CFU/100 cm2 set for ISS. Fungal levels were occasionally above the Russian standard of 100 CFU/100 cm2 as well as above the US standard of 400 CFU/100 cm2 set for ISS once. Bacteria recovered from the commander's seat post #1 (site 1) included <i>Vibrio alginolyticus</i> on FD54. <i>Stenotrophomonas maltophilia</i> was isolated from the dining table (site 2) on FD28; an unidentified Gram negative rod was recovered from this location was on FD54. <i>Rhodotorula glutinis</i>, a yeast, was isolated from the handle of the treadmill (site 4) on FD100. The bacterium <i>Pasteurella haemolytica</i> was recovered from the Kristall Module hatch (site 5) on FD54.</p>
<p>NASA 7, STS-89, and STS-91 <br />
Mir bacterial levels frequently exceeded the Russian standard of 1000 CFU/100 cm2 as well as the US standard of 4000 CFU/100 cm2 set for ISS. Fungal levels were occasionally above the Russian standard of 100 CFU/100 cm2 as well as the US standard of 400 CFU/100 cm2 set for ISS. Bacteria recovered from the commander's seat post #1 included <i>Serratia marcescens</i> and <i>Stenotrophomonas maltophilia</i> on FD78, and <i>Pasteurella haemolytica</i> on FD135. The fungi <i>Aspergillus niger</i> was isolated from this site on FD43; yeast recovered from this location included <i>Candida parapsilosis</i> on FD78 and an unidentified yeast on FD135. <i>Pseudomonas stutzeri</i> and <i>Serratia marcescens</i> were isolated from the dining table on FD43; other microorganisms recovered from this location were <i>Klebsiella pneumoniae</i> and <i>Rhodotorula rubra</i> on FD78, and <i>Serratia fonticola</i> on FD119. From the commander's cabin wall, <i>Serratia liquefaciens</i> was recovered on FD43 and <i>Aspergillus niger</i> on FD78. Bacteria isolated from the handle of the treadmill included <i>Sphingomonas paucimobilis</i> on FD43; <i>Aspergillus niger</i> was recovered on FD43. <i>Serratia liquefaciens</i> and <i>Aspergillus niger</i> were recovered again from the Kristall Module hatch on FD43. <br />
Onboard the Shuttle, <i>Aspergillus niger</i>, <i>Candida parapsilosis</i>, and <i>Candida guilliermondii</i> were recovered from the half-moon shaped air vent above the Waste Control System during the docked phase of STS-89. Yeast recovered from the left-most air return in the port side crawl-through included <i>Candida guilliermondii</i> during the docked phase of the STS-89. <i>Acinetobacter lwofii</i> was recovered from the half-moon shaped air vent above the Waste Control System during the sampling session performed 26 days before the launch of STS-91. Yeast recovered from the left-most air return in the port side crawl-through included <i>Candida guilliermondii</i> and <i>Candida parapsilosis</i> during the docked phase of STS-91. <i>Acinetobacter lwofii</i> was recovered from the half-moon shaped vent for the Commander's chair in the Flight Deck one day before the launch of STS-91.</p>
<p>WATER MICROBIOLOGY <br />
NASA 3 and STS-79 <br />
All but one of the Mir hot water samples had no bacterial growth, indicating that the rigorous treatment train and final heat-pasteurization step of the Mir system is well able to maintain the quality of water from this source at standards well below the US and Russian maximum limits. <i>Burkholderia cepacia</i> was the only bacterial contaminant recovered from the Shuttle potable water system; this organism is frequently recovered from this system. Tank B represents pre-treated water, and predictably contained many bacteria.</p>
<p>NASA 4 and STS-81<br />
All Mir hot water samples were within the US acceptability limits set for the International Space Station. All but one of the Mir hot water samples had no bacterial growth, indicating that the treatment train and final heat-pasteurization step of the Mir system is able to maintain the microbial quality of water from this source at standards well below both the US (100 CFU/100 ml) and Russian (10,000 CFU/100 ml) limits. Cold and SVO-ZV samples predictably contained many bacteria.</p>
<p>NASA 5 and STS-84 <br />
The in-flight monitoring that was to be performed on the Mir on FD15, FD55, FD85, FD115, and FD135 was cancelled due to the Spektr accident. The sample collection kits, as well as archived samples, were stowed within the Spektr module at the time of the accident. No further sampling or the return of samples was possible. Pre and postflight samples from STS-84 were analyzed. <br />
All Mir hot water samples were within the US acceptability limits set for the International Space Station. The one Mir hot water sample had a bacterial level of only 20 CFU/100 ml, indicating that the treatment train and final heat-pasteurization step of the Mir system is able to maintain the microbial quality of water from this source at standards well below both the US (100 CFU/100 ml) and Russian (10,000 CFU/100 ml) limits. Cold and SVO-ZV samples predictably contained many bacteria.</p>
<p>NASA 6 and STS-86 <br />
None of the Mir hot water samples had bacterial growth, indicating that the treatment train and final heat-pasteurization step of the Mir system is able to maintain the microbial quality of water from this source at standards well below both the US (100 CFU/100 ml) and Russian (10,000 CFU/100 ml) limits. SVO-ZV samples predictably contained a moderate number of bacteria.</p>
<p>NASA 7, STS-89, and STS-91 <br />
None of the Mir hot water samples had bacterial growth, indicating that the treatment train and final heat-pasteurization step of the Mir system is able to maintain the microbial quality of water from this source at standards well below both the US (100 CFU/100 ml) and Russian (10,000 CFU/100 ml) limits. Cold and SVO-ZV samples predictably contained many bacteria. <br />
During the first water sampling session of the mission, the NASA 7 crewmember reported the presence of "brown specks" in the unprocessed sample. The crewmember was instructed to proceed with the sample processing and retain the sample collection bag for return to ground. All of the samples that were collected during the mission were returned to Earth. Analysis was based on the descriptions given by the crewmember during debrief, by visual inspection of the sample collection bags which were returned to ground, and by chemical analysis of the discolored water. Upon inspection of the bags, laboratory personnel did not observe any type of particulate present. However, in four of five bags that were returned, there was a small amount of discolored water in one of the two valve stems present on the bags. Chemical analysis showed that the water was found to contain sodium thiosulfate. Each sample bag contains a small amount of sodium thiosulfate, a chemical that is added to the bags before flight to neutralize the biocide (silver) used in the Russian water reclamation system. The cause of this observation noted by the crewmember has not been conclusively determined. One possible cause could have been inadequate temperature control of the Water Experiment Kit. It is known that elevated temperatures will cause a yellowish-brown discoloration of this compound.</p>
<p>FOOD PACKET <br />
The microorganisms present seem to be of environmental origin, rather than contamination of the food.</p>
<p>'FREE' SURFACE CONDENSATE <br />
NASA 6 <br />
A sample of 'free' surface condensate was collected on FD113 and returned to ground on STS-89 for complete analyses. Upon visual examination of the sample, the following observations were noted: the sample was cloudy with a brown particulate matter present. A wet mount preparation resulted in the rare observation of amoeba (Figure 1) and ciliated protozoa (Figure 2). Scanning Electron Microscopy revealed the presence of rod- and spirochete-form bacteria (Figure 3). Selected staining techniques resulted in no ova or parasites being observed. Culturing was performed using selective media for the detection of aerobes and fungi. A wide variety of bacteria were present in the samples. The bacteria isolated were of types that will grow in different environmental conditions and can tolerate a wide temperature range. Members of the <i>Enterobacteriaceae</i> family (organisms of enteric origin) that were isolated, such as the genera <i>Serratia</i> and <i>Yersinia</i>, can colonize sites in healthy hosts. The presence of these organisms is noteworthy in that they are opportunistic pathogens and can cause disease in immuno-compromised hosts. Other bacteria that were isolated, such as <i>Flavobacterium meningosepticum</i>, are rarely of clinical significance but may occasionally cause wound infections.</p>
<p>NASA 7 <br />
Two samples of 'free' surface condensate were collected on FD127 and returned to ground on STS-91 for complete analyses. Upon visual examination of each sample, the following observations were noted: the "slimy" sample was cloudy with a brown particulate matter present; the "fresh" sample was slightly cloudy with a white particulate matter present. A wet mount preparation resulted in the rare observation of dust mites and showed the presence of a few ciliated protozoa. Scanning Electron Microscopy revealed the presence of rod- and spirochete-form bacteria. Selected staining techniques resulted in no ova or parasites being observed. Culturing was performed using selective media for the detection of aerobes, anaerobes, fungi, Legionella spp., and mycobacteria. A wide variety of bacteria were present in both samples. The bacteria isolated were of types that will grow in different environmental conditions and can tolerate a wide temperature range. Members of the <i>Enterobacteriaceae</i> family (organisms of enteric origin) that were isolated, such as the genera <i>Enterobacter</i>, <i>Citrobacter</i>, <i>Serratia</i> and <i>Yersinia</i>, can colonize sites in healthy hosts. The presence of these organisms is noteworthy in that they are opportunistic pathogens and can cause disease in immuno-compromised hosts. Although a presumptive identification of <i>Legionella spp.</i> was reported, final pneumophila species identification was not confirmed. Other bacteria that were isolated, such as <i>Comamonas spp.</i>, <i>Flavobacterium meningosepticus</i> and <i>Pseudomonas fluorescens</i>, are rarely of clinical significance but may occasionally cause wound infections.</p>
<p>CONCLUSIONS: <br />
Overall, the microbiota of all crewmembers sampled was characteristic of healthy individuals. When present the fungal isolates recovered from specimens generally represented common environmental contaminants. No significant changes were found during spaceflight or upon return to earth relative to preflight results.</p>
<p>Amounts of microorganisms observed in the air and on surfaces of the Shuttle and Mir station were mostly within the acceptability limits set by the US for the ISS. The types of organisms recovered were consistent with previous findings from both Shuttle and Mir missions. Fungal levels of the air samples collected on the Mir were often above the limits set for this station; however, no major adverse health effects were seen in the crewmembers. The temporal fluctuations in levels of organisms probably reflect changes in environmental characteristics, such as temperature and humidity, at the time of sample collection.</p>
<p>The bacterial load of the Shuttle water samples were consistent with those seen on previous missions. All Mir galley hot water samples were within the standards set for both the Mir and the ISS. All isolates were consistent with findings from previous Shuttle and Mir missions.</p>
<p>The hardware designed for in-flight microbial monitoring performed nominally.</p>
<p>Collection and examination of 'free' surface condensate can prove to be a valuable tool in assessing the overall condition of the health of a closed environmental system such as a space station.</p>
<p>Although many potential pathogens were isolated from the surfaces, air and "free" surface condensate, it is interesting to note that the microbial flora of the crewmembers remained that of healthy individuals and that these microorganisms imposed no adverse health effects. <end></end></p>
